1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,679
[話者A] Hi, it's very nice to meet you.

2
00:00:03,679 --> 00:00:04,980
[話者B] It's nice to meet you too.

3
00:00:06,639 --> 00:00:09,859
[話者A] Well, I'm very happy to be here, it's very nice to meet you.

4
00:00:09,919 --> 00:00:15,804
[話者B] Yeah, I'm also happy to meet you and especially in this location in the Freud Museum. I think it's the perfect place

5
00:00:16,045 --> 00:00:16,204
[話者A] Yes.

6
00:00:16,364 --> 00:00:34,200
[話者B] To talk about art and creativity and the human mind and, you know, in this age of of AI, when all of these things are in in question, And what what will be the future of of artists, of human creativity?

7
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:37,799
[話者B] But maybe we'll start with something a bit lighter.

8
00:00:38,739 --> 00:01:00,000
[話者A] Yeah. There's a lot we can dive into. But when I first got the offer to do this talk with you, I was very excited because my son turns 10 this month. And when I was pregnant ten years ago, my very good friend gave me your book. Oh. Sapiens.

9
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:00,880
[話者A] Okay.

10
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:10,159
[話者A] With a note saying, since you're growing a new Sapiens, I'd like to gift you this book. And so it felt very full circle.

11
00:01:12,254 --> 00:01:18,674
[話者B] And was there anything in the book that kind of caught your attention? A story, an idea, something that...

12
00:01:19,694 --> 00:01:41,465
[話者A] I'm sure a lot of people say this, but the part where you share the perspective that cultivating wheat, we didn't domesticate wheat, they domesticated humans. That was one of those like, oh my god, like world like view shifting moments that,

13
00:01:42,084 --> 00:01:43,545
[話者A] I don't think I'll ever forget.

14
00:01:44,965 --> 00:01:48,185
[話者A] And I really enjoyed those moments in Nexus as well.

15
00:01:48,325 --> 00:01:49,144
[話者B] Thank you.

16
00:01:50,084 --> 00:02:03,619
[話者A] Like, I love the way you express... You take the way you leave all these ideas and then like a mathematical equation, you somehow bring it together into a very simple expression.

17
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:14,504
[話者A] Like, I love the analogy of the world wide web having spread and connected people, which you think might be compared to a cocoon.

18
00:02:15,604 --> 00:02:18,965
[話者A] I love I love making... I love those connections you make.

19
00:02:18,965 --> 00:02:26,389
[話者B] Yeah. I think that previously, I mean, everybody expected the web to just connect all all all the universe ultimately.

20
00:02:26,849 --> 00:02:30,949
[話者B] And now increasingly, the kind of the web is closing in on on us.

21
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:43,634
[話者B] And people sometimes live side by side, each one cocooned in a different universe, almost unable to to to reach out and to connect to the next person.

22
00:02:44,655 --> 00:02:54,550
[話者B] And I think, you know, when when I think about music, I mean, this is what music has been trying to do for thousands of years. This is its power.

23
00:02:54,610 --> 00:02:55,349
[話者A] And art.

24
00:02:55,409 --> 00:02:57,169
[話者B] Yeah. To to connect people together.

25
00:02:57,169 --> 00:02:57,669
[話者A] Yeah.

26
00:02:58,370 --> 00:03:02,209
[話者B] And I was wondering what our... Maybe we we just jump right in.

27
00:03:02,209 --> 00:03:03,430
[話者A] Sure. Let's do it.

28
00:03:04,004 --> 00:03:17,590
[話者B] What do you think is the future of music in the in the era of AI? Like, maybe I'll just say about myself. I I write books. I'm a scholar. But writing history is somewhere in between kind of science and art.

29
00:03:17,969 --> 00:03:21,830
[話者B] Because to write a history book, it's not an equation. You need to tell stories.

30
00:03:22,370 --> 00:03:26,870
[話者B] So I also, to some extent, I I I see myself as an artist.

31
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:46,139
[話者B] And I don't know if I'll still be writing books in five years or ten years because maybe by the 2030s, AIs will write books like Sapiens and Nexus so much better than me that it will be kind of almost meaningless for a human being to engage in that.

32
00:03:47,419 --> 00:03:50,719
[話者A] I just find that really hard to imagine.

33
00:03:51,740 --> 00:04:05,924
[話者A] I don't... I understand logically that that is where things are going in a lot of creative fields. Like with, I was talking with my friend who's in the film industry and she said everyone's talking about VO3.

34
00:04:06,384 --> 00:04:07,485
[話者A] It's the new...

35
00:04:07,485 --> 00:04:08,805
[話者B] Oh, it's new. Yeah.

36
00:04:09,104 --> 00:04:09,185
[話者B] Yeah.

37
00:04:09,185 --> 00:04:14,084
[話者A] And it's just another level. It made a big jump in terms of what it could do.

38
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:23,279
[話者A] And people are really, well, some people are quite shocked, shaken by their future, the future of their, you know, their work.

39
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:42,294
[話者A] I understand that logically, my thinking part, but something, something deep inside me makes me, think there's no way humans will not want human made things.

40
00:04:43,235 --> 00:04:49,439
[話者A] I mean I think we're, we're just naturally art making people.

41
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:54,720
[話者A] It's one of the first things we do when we, leave the womb.

42
00:04:55,180 --> 00:05:09,884
[話者A] We start, we can't talk but we're like sort of singing and babbling And we can't even walk yet, but you sort of dance a bit or tap things or react to things like that.

43
00:05:12,105 --> 00:05:19,439
[話者A] And I think the universe is full of music. That's where it comes from. And art. I mean, I think it's basically...

44
00:05:19,819 --> 00:05:30,435
[話者A] I, I also... I love, the part where you said... You were talking about how AI... When AI beat, AlphaGo beat a human player. Yeah.

45
00:05:30,915 --> 00:05:50,379
[話者A] And you were saying that, creativity... Well, the creative jobs are not safe either because creativity, in simple terms, you said creativity is, well, one of the big aspects of it is pattern recognition and, or, and also... And breaking the pattern. Exactly.

46
00:05:50,379 --> 00:05:54,319
[話者B] Yes. You recognize the pattern and you break it. And that's the creativity.

47
00:05:55,099 --> 00:06:19,370
[話者B] And in many fields, again, I I would love to hear your views on music because I I... I'm not really an expert in music. But certainly in games like Go and chess, now AI is far more creative than humans. You know, when you have this kind of humans only competition and there is always this fear that somebody's getting advice from an AI, one of the ways they check it is the level of creativity

48
00:06:19,829 --> 00:06:21,129
[話者B] that the players display.

49
00:06:21,615 --> 00:06:29,394
[話者B] If they play in a way which is too creative, immediately the suspicion is, oh, they must be getting advice from an AI.

50
00:06:30,175 --> 00:06:35,550
[話者B] Now with with music, with film, or with writing books, we are still not there, of course.

51
00:06:36,669 --> 00:06:43,009
[話者B] But when I look at the trend over the last ten years, you know, if I think what do I do when I write a book?

52
00:06:43,629 --> 00:07:01,414
[話者B] So first of all, I need to come up with a subject, like a question that really bothers me. Then I need to gather a lot of material, and I need to analyze it. And then I need to kind of put it in writing with... I'm using stories and anecdotes and so forth. So these are the basic stages.

53
00:07:01,990 --> 00:07:13,850
[話者B] And when I look at each one of these stages, I can't see why AI will not be able to do that. For a long time, I had friends who say they will never be able to come up with the questions.

54
00:07:14,794 --> 00:07:25,935
[話者B] But now I have colleagues who go to the AI to Oh my god. Give them what are the most interesting research questions within field x.

55
00:07:26,490 --> 00:07:32,009
[話者A] But that's not just asking them to do research, that's them coming up with the questions, or are they looking for...

56
00:07:32,009 --> 00:07:34,410
[話者A] They they ask the AI To think of questions?

57
00:07:34,649 --> 00:07:46,625
[話者B] To come up with what are the kind of main kind of missing links, The things it would be very interesting to know and that we still don't know about a certain field or a certain subject.

58
00:07:47,085 --> 00:07:51,105
[話者B] And similarly, we we certainly gathering information and analyzing it.

59
00:07:51,485 --> 00:07:57,709
[話者B] You the amount of information that I can gather is is infinitesimal compared to to the AI.

60
00:07:58,089 --> 00:08:02,670
[話者B] And then all the way to writing, I'm using stories or anecdotes.

61
00:08:03,290 --> 00:08:08,914
[話者B] So I've already seen, you know, these wonderful stories written by AI.

62
00:08:09,454 --> 00:08:32,259
[話者B] And I agree that humans will always want to create for the joy of creating, as children do. But would it still be valued? I mean, until today, it's not just that we liked doing it, it was extremely valuable. Nothing on planet Earth could be as creative as as us.

63
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:38,419
[話者B] And now in more and more fields, there is something which can be more creative.

64
00:08:39,514 --> 00:08:52,254
[話者B] And and lastly, I also really kind of connect to what you said that I can understand it in in my intellect, but deep inside, I I I tend to think about it as a kind of brain stomach barrier.

65
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:59,659
[話者B] But my brain already understands what is happening, but my stomach refuses to digest it.

66
00:09:00,759 --> 00:09:06,539
[話者B] And the stomach is more powerful. I mean, ultimately, we are controlled by the stomach, not not by the brain.

67
00:09:07,154 --> 00:09:11,174
[話者A] And our instincts seem to lie a lot in our gut feelings.

68
00:09:11,475 --> 00:09:19,174
[話者B] Yeah. Our gut feelings are kind of deep, kind of emotional reactions. I think that they come from from from the stomach, like, literally.

69
00:09:19,490 --> 00:09:25,509
[話者B] I mean, there are hundreds of millions of neurons in the stomach. It's not just... It's just just a metaphor.

70
00:09:26,289 --> 00:09:39,144
[話者B] And so I can I can say these things and I give talks about it and interviews, but when I look at how I actually live my life, I still can't really take it seriously?

71
00:09:39,845 --> 00:09:42,824
[話者B] And it's true of of most of my colleagues like that they...

72
00:09:43,365 --> 00:09:44,584
[話者B] In the AI field.

73
00:09:45,044 --> 00:10:00,610
[話者B] Like, they will come up with the most kind of scary or far reaching predictions about what AI will do, and then in the way they actually live, they don't change anything because it's too, it's, we can't really imagine it.

74
00:10:01,070 --> 00:10:09,004
[話者A] Well, it's like, I guess we're, we're quite slow. We're not very good at adapting to something that hasn't really happened yet.

75
00:10:09,705 --> 00:10:18,924
[話者A] We know that climate change is real, but we're not really dealing with it very well. So maybe we'll just have to see how it goes.

76
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:29,259
[話者A] But, I I do understand that being creative is a lot of problem solving. That's mainly how I see it.

77
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:52,779
[話者A] Well, raising a question, like you said. And then, thinking of finding problems is kind of how I view it. And then finding ways to solve the problem. And then a big part of it is also understanding patterns and breaking it in interesting ways, or ways that feel good. I like to explain that when I make music

78
00:10:53,480 --> 00:11:07,365
[話者A] as, it's like a dance in how I feel, where it's going, or a balance of things that feel natural and expected, like a melody or chords.

79
00:11:07,904 --> 00:11:18,004
[話者A] Well, chords are a very good way of, maybe a good way of thinking about how AI could make music very well.

80
00:11:18,639 --> 00:11:25,139
[話者A] Because I mean if you open, if you open like a music theory book, you just see a bunch of numbers basically.

81
00:11:25,759 --> 00:11:41,245
[話者A] There are patterns like, you know, the famous, like you start with a dominant chord, which is five, and then you resolve it in a tonic chord, which is one, and patterns like four five one, and two five one, or things like that.

82
00:11:41,865 --> 00:11:46,365
[話者A] So it feels very mathematical and structured, and almost objective.

83
00:11:48,550 --> 00:11:50,330
[話者A] But what I... But...

84
00:11:50,790 --> 00:12:11,254
[話者A] So of all the musicians, I guess in a way I'm thinking AI is also another sea of musicians that have a different approach. But if I almost think of them as just having more musicians in the world, then I would still just keep doing what I do, which is

85
00:12:11,715 --> 00:12:25,590
[話者A] I listen to the music that's out there, or that's been made in the past, that I love, or maybe don't like. And I come up with the natural parts, that feel natural and good to me as a human, or just as a listener.

86
00:12:26,285 --> 00:12:37,485
[話者A] And then I like to throw in parts that are like a betrayal, to the listener, where it's a bit unexpected, or it's a bit weird, like that that shouldn't happen. So it's breaking the pattern. Yes.

87
00:12:39,085 --> 00:12:41,429
[話者A] That's that's the most fun part of it.

88
00:12:41,909 --> 00:12:45,690
[話者A] But I base it on what feels interesting for me.

89
00:12:46,070 --> 00:13:09,084
[話者A] So I'm just curious. I wonder, without a clear objective, I think the biggest difference in the creative process as far as I can see with my limited knowledge of AI is that my objective in making music would be completely egotistical because it's, it's just me trying to discover things about me or the world around me.

90
00:13:11,639 --> 00:13:16,059
[話者B] What do you discover through music? Can you give an example of like something you discovered?

91
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:43,470
[話者A] There's always something quite big that it's almost like I... Because if I if I don't, especially with lyrics, maybe this is where it comes in more than the musical part of it, but if I don't come up with something that I find is new to me, like a new idea, something I've been rolling in the back of my mind, a question I've had, and I'm stuck. I can't come up with the last line in the song, but I feel like it's the most important part

92
00:13:43,769 --> 00:13:57,184
[話者A] and then I work on it more, push push, keep asking myself and then I'll come up with like the the web versus cocoon sort of analogy and maybe that becomes the title, webin cocoon. Or...

93
00:13:57,324 --> 00:14:09,019
[話者B] How do you actually work on it? Like if you if you're missing the last line of of of the song. Yeah. What does... I mean what do you do to work on it?

94
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:16,700
[話者A] So it's another aspect of creativity that I think is really important, which is patience and also letting go.

95
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:44,289
[話者A] And I, I like to compare this patience part to... I feel like a fisherman sitting on a boat and no matter what I'm doing, if I'm working on lyrics or maybe even just the music part of a song, I'm I'm sitting on a boat in the middle of the ocean and whether I'm having dinner with, making, cooking, or getting changed, or just watching TV, or talking with a friend, I'm, in the back of my mind, I'm sitting on the boat.

96
00:14:44,429 --> 00:14:45,070
[話者B] Waiting for...

97
00:14:45,070 --> 00:14:50,884
[話者A] Because I know that I can't force it. I have to, part of me just has to be patient and believe that it will come.

98
00:14:51,585 --> 00:14:52,565
[話者A] And then it does.

99
00:14:53,024 --> 00:14:58,404
[話者A] And have enough distractions around me so that my mind is not laser focused on just that issue.

100
00:14:58,784 --> 00:15:01,365
[話者A] Because the ideas really bubble up to the surface.

101
00:15:02,029 --> 00:15:07,090
[話者A] Like the subconscious ideas when I'm well distracted.

102
00:15:08,350 --> 00:15:27,985
[話者B] But is it just one fish? Or or I mean, when I try to think about the metaphor, like, is it like an empty ocean and I'm waiting for this one fish to to to to bite? Or is it that every second, one fish or the other bites and most of them are just, no, not this one, not this one,

103
00:15:28,539 --> 00:15:30,639
[話者B] And until the the right fish bites.

104
00:15:31,100 --> 00:15:42,639
[話者B] Like, is it like lots of noise in in the mind until out of the noise something, yes, this is the thing? Or is it just like silence, silence, silence, and then something?

105
00:15:43,815 --> 00:15:51,035
[話者A] A lot of things bubble up and then I think, no, that's not quite it. Maybe once I think, oh, that's, that sounds good.

106
00:15:51,654 --> 00:15:56,970
[話者A] And then a bit later I think, no, wait, no, not yet. That's not the thing that I was waiting for.

107
00:15:57,850 --> 00:15:59,230
[話者A] And and then when something...

108
00:15:59,850 --> 00:16:15,605
[話者A] I just know it when it really truly captures this like far away, out of focus vision, the the answer that I was sort of searching for in my in my self, which could be something as basic as what I have been feeling about something.

109
00:16:16,144 --> 00:16:28,820
[話者A] It's like a way of discovering some sort of truth about myself, which I believe if it comes from deep within me enough and it's honest enough, it's a universal truth. So it'll...

110
00:16:29,679 --> 00:16:34,259
[話者A] Pretty much anyone can relate to it because it's just that basic.

111
00:16:34,879 --> 00:16:35,379
[話者B] Mhmm.

112
00:16:36,879 --> 00:16:40,815
[話者A] And I love the ending of your acknowledgments and Nexus.

113
00:16:41,355 --> 00:16:42,654
[話者A] I think it was the acknowledgments.

114
00:16:43,035 --> 00:16:43,615
[話者A] Oh, okay.

115
00:16:44,475 --> 00:16:52,335
[話者A] Where you said your book, when I'm reading your book, that's when our minds connect and so the book itself is is a Nexus.

116
00:16:53,460 --> 00:17:02,200
[話者A] And that's the thing, that's the truth that makes me feel connected to myself and thus to everyone in the world.

117
00:17:03,940 --> 00:17:11,335
[話者A] So, but the motivation, I guess, is what I'm trying to say, the biggest difference is. But I don't know how AI feel.

118
00:17:11,634 --> 00:17:25,289
[話者A] So I'm just curious if AI, without giving an objective like make music that will grab people's attention, Or make music that will facilitate this specific sort of bodily reaction.

119
00:17:25,829 --> 00:17:32,890
[話者A] Like sweating or elevated heart rate or maybe even deeper things in the brain like chemicals being released and analyzed.

120
00:17:34,365 --> 00:17:36,224
[話者A] That's about the result.

121
00:17:36,445 --> 00:17:36,945
[話者B] And

122
00:17:38,605 --> 00:17:41,984
[話者A] winning a match that has a clear result, it's aiming for.

123
00:17:42,365 --> 00:17:51,519
[話者A] But when I'm making something, I actually, it's like I'm trying to find that, I don't know what the result is until I get there.

124
00:17:53,259 --> 00:17:55,119
[話者B] What is the result for for you?

125
00:17:55,579 --> 00:17:58,000
[話者A] Just the piece of music, but it's almost like a byproduct.

126
00:17:59,099 --> 00:18:06,704
[話者A] And everything important that happened to me, the value of the song is what happened while I was making it. Yeah.

127
00:18:06,704 --> 00:18:19,519
[話者B] But this is for us as creators or as... I mean, this I completely understand. I I agree that there is a deep need yearning in humans to create, and this will continue irrespective of AI.

128
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:20,240
[話者A] Yeah.

129
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:23,900
[話者A] So... But whether people will appreciate that or not.

130
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:24,299
[話者A] Right? Yeah.

131
00:18:24,299 --> 00:18:31,815
[話者B] Whether people will appreciate it, whether it will have a place in in the world and it won't just be my hobby to do it.

132
00:18:32,355 --> 00:18:33,335
[話者B] That's the question.

133
00:18:34,035 --> 00:18:43,015
[話者B] And when... A lot of time when you ask artists or or or, creators, what are you try... What is the result? What are we trying to achieve?

134
00:18:44,090 --> 00:18:53,390
[話者B] So one very common answer is that they are trying to create a certain state of mind in somebody else.

135
00:18:53,849 --> 00:18:58,875
[話者B] To bring up a certain emotion, Joy or sadness or whatever.

136
00:18:59,894 --> 00:19:05,654
[話者B] And this is the kind of thing that potentially AI would would be very good at.

137
00:19:05,654 --> 00:19:06,375
[話者A] Yes. Yeah.

138
00:19:06,375 --> 00:19:28,974
[話者B] Even if it has no emotions of its own, it will be able to recognize the emotion of other people. And then find by trial and error how if you imagine, like, if you compare a human being, like, our emotional system is basically like a piano, that the AI will learn how to play the human emotional piano

139
00:19:30,394 --> 00:19:47,580
[話者B] and how to produce sadness, how to produce joy, how to produce fear or awe, better than a human artist simply through the sheer volume of information it can process.

140
00:19:48,600 --> 00:20:01,414
[話者B] Like, it can it can gather data on millions and millions of actual human beings. Like when I write a book, so I would sometimes try to imagine my reader.

141
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:15,700
[話者B] And I usually base my imagined reader either on myself or on people I actually know or on encounters I had with students at university or public events or interviews.

142
00:20:16,080 --> 00:20:21,855
[話者B] But this is a very limited pool of examples, of data.

143
00:20:22,794 --> 00:20:30,494
[話者B] So when I'm doing it, maybe I have a kind of a pool of information on a couple of 100 people.

144
00:20:31,399 --> 00:20:39,740
[話者B] The AI that would write the book or the piece of music, it would have data on millions and billions of human beings over time.

145
00:20:40,519 --> 00:20:54,095
[話者B] So if the goal is to create a certain state of mind for other people, I just don't see how we can compete with the AI on that.

146
00:20:54,474 --> 00:21:04,150
[話者B] Especially as it will also have the advantage of being able to tailor the the book, the songs specifically to different people.

147
00:21:04,930 --> 00:21:12,710
[話者B] I can't write a slightly different book for different people, but for AI maybe it would be quite easy.

148
00:21:13,174 --> 00:21:18,154
[話者A] Yeah. I think the tailor making aspect is quite interesting. I'd love to know...

149
00:21:18,694 --> 00:21:33,480
[話者A] I'd love to try it and see if it'll make music that will eventually, I guess, the kind of music that I really love. I mean, it's almost like music streaming platforms suggesting playlists and songs based on what you've listened to algorithmically.

150
00:21:35,620 --> 00:21:37,320
